WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.920
Pick up a receiver and you're interacting
with humanity's oldest

2
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.200
real-time communication interface, the
telephone.

3
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.760
It is entirely uncompromising.

4
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:12.880
In a text box, artificial intelligence has
a place to

5
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.040
hide. Latency is masked by a blinking
typing indicator, buying

6
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:19.760
the machine a few seconds to process its
next move

7
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.440
while you sit and wait.

8
00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.320
But on a live voice line, that luxury of
time

9
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.200
disappears. You expect an immediate
response, an unbroken physical presence

10
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:30.800
on the other end of the line.

11
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.520
A delayed response in a chat window is
standard.

12
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.840
A delayed response on a phone call feels
like a

13
00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:40.320
broken connection. The environment
actively punishes slow processing.

14
00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.520
This creates what engineers call the
three-second rule.

15
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.800
Three seconds of dead air is the exact
psychological threshold

16
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.840
where silence turns into a perceived
system failure.

17
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.960
When standard AI models are forced into
this constraint, they

18
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.080
buckle. They stutter, freeze mid-sentence,
or frantically hallucinate responses just

19
00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.440
to fill the dead air.

20
00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:02.120
The human caller notices the gap.

21
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.080
They assume the system is broken or
stupid, and they

22
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:08.360
hang up. Trust is lost instantly.

23
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.480
This is why simply plugging a large
language model into

24
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.480
a microphone doesn't work.

25
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.360
Surviving this tight temporal window
requires a dedicated engineering platform

26
00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.760
like Telthresis. In this environment, low
latency is not a

27
00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:25.320
minor performance metric you optimize
later.

28
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.680
It is an absolute survival threshold for
any AI placed

29
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.800
on a phone line.

30
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:35.560
Telthresis stands for Telephony-Linked
Embedded LLM System for Interactive Support.

31
00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.280
It is a framework built to solve that
exact failure

32
00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:42.920
mode, absence. The sequence to hold a
conversation is strict,

33
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:47.240
convert spoken words to data in
speech-to-text, formulate a response

34
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.720
during LLM reasoning, and generate an
audible voice via text-to-speech.

35
00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.600
The reality of a live call means all three
consecutive

36
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.760
phases must complete in under three
seconds.

37
00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.760
By explicitly optimizing the entire
pipeline to hit this specific

38
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.520
threshold, the system eliminates the dead
air and nervous stuttering

39
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.000
that typically ruins traditional voice
bots.

40
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.560
You cannot fake real-time conversation.

41
00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:15.040
Defeating absence requires engineering the
cognitive loop to physically outpace

42
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.960
human impatience, creating a machine that
feels truly present.

43
00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.840
Once an AI is fast enough to speak, it
encounters

44
00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.560
a second major failure mode, amnesia.

45
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.120
This is when the system loses track of the
conversation

46
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.880
or starts making things up.

47
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:32.720
The standard approach forces conversation
history and API data into

48
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.480
a single working memory block.

49
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.880
The container overloads and collapses.

50
00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:41.520
To fix this, Tell3Sys uses tri-layer
memory, partitioning its recall

51
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.960
functions across Redis, SQLite, and vector
storage.

52
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:48.080
This separation lets the agent pull live
data from external

53
00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.280
sources, like calendars, local weather
APIs, and user emails, and

54
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:55.320
operates without overwhelming its
immediate working memory.

55
00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.720
By organizing exactly how the machine
stores and retrieves data,

56
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.960
you secure long-term conversational
persistence and remove the risk of

57
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.240
hallucination. The final and most common
failure mode is hubris.

58
00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.960
This is the tendency of an AI voice system
to

59
00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.880
confidently attempt a complex task that it
has no ability

60
00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:17.480
to actually complete.

61
00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.200
Tell Thrisa's safety oracle acts as an
embedded pre-execution filter,

62
00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:26.600
actively evaluating every planned action
before the AI speaks.

63
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.760
When the conversation exceeds safe
parameters, the oracle blocks the

64
00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.520
action, immediately redirecting the call
to a human operator with

65
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.000
full context. Handoffs are often omitted
by voice platforms because

66
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.960
engineers treat them as an admission of
defeat.

67
00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:45.920
Here, the handoff is engineered as a
deliberate, necessary safety

68
00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.800
boundary. An AI proves its sophistication
not through endless trivia,

69
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.000
but through its ability to recognize
strict limits and safely

70
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.400
admit defeat. Tellthresis demonstrates a
necessary shift in how we

71
00:03:58.400 --> 00:03:59.760
build these tools.

72
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.560
Artificial intelligence requires strict,
human-centric constraints if it is going

73
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:07.040
to function reliably in the real world.

74
00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.600
Simply throwing more processing power or
increasingly massive language models

75
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.880
at a problem will result in a broken user
experience

76
00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.320
if the system lacks real-time
architectural boundaries.

77
00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.080
As these automated agents continue to
handle our daily schedules

78
00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.760
and communications, the ultimate metric
for their success is going

79
00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:29.200
to be reliability, not just raw
intelligence.

80
00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:32.320
The most effective voice AI will be the
one engineered

81
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.600
to be fast enough to feel entirely present
and honest

82
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.480
enough to step aside when it shouldn't be.

